  "image_name": "115d3dfd6ec5e396dde41b1938ef11cb.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several surface-functionalization strategies for coating a rectangular particle, likely a nanocrystal or DNA-origami-like core, with different biomolecular or polymer shells. Across the panels, the same grey block is modified by attaching branching DNA motifs, lipid membranes, polycations, peptides, dendrons, peptoids, or silica-forming reagents.\n\nA main theme is the creation of increasingly complex outer layers around a rigid core. Some approaches build dense nucleic-acid coronas, such as HSA–DNA dendrites or dendritic oligonucleotides, which generate a highly branched shell. Another route embeds the core inside a PEGylated lipid bilayer via liposome reconstitution, producing a membrane-like coating. Other panels show electrostatic or covalent polymer coatings: polycation shells made from LPEI or chitosan, oligolysine–PEG adsorption, and glutaraldehyde crosslinking of free amines to stabilize the surface.\n\nThe lower row expands this idea to more elaborate molecular architectures, including BSA-linked dendrons and DNA peptoids bearing multiple charged side chains. The final panel shows inorganic encapsulation by first using silane primers such as APTES or TMAPS, then depositing TEOS-derived silica. Overall, the figure compares a toolbox of organic, biomolecular, lipid, and inorganic methods for wrapping and stabilizing the same particle scaffold.",
